WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.277
Idea. Your arm moves because muscles pull on bones. Here is the upper arm. The bone is the humerus. The biceps is the muscle at the front. The triceps is the muscle at the back. The elbow is a hinge joint: it bends in one plane only, like a door.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:33.732
A muscle can only pull. It cannot push. When a muscle contracts, it gets shorter and pulls. So two muscles are needed: one to bend the arm, one to straighten it. They are an antagonistic pair: when one contracts, the other relaxes.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:47.306
Method. Step one: decide the movement. To raise the forearm, which means bending the arm, the biceps contracts. It gets shorter and fatter. At the same time, the triceps relaxes.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:01:04.035
Step two: follow the pull. A tendon is a tough cord that joins a muscle to a bone. The biceps pulls on its tendon. The tendon does not stretch, so it pulls the radius, a bone in the forearm. The forearm turns at the elbow and goes up.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:16.256
To lower the forearm, which means straightening the arm, it is the other way round. The triceps contracts and pulls on the ulna, the other forearm bone. The biceps relaxes.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:25.866
Step three: use the key words in your answer. Contracts and relaxes. Antagonistic pair. Tendon. And the bone that is pulled.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:41.856
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The diagram shows two muscles on the upper arm. The front muscle runs down to the forearm. The question asks how these muscles help to raise the forearm. Four marks.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:52.827
Raise means bend, so start with the biceps. Mark one: the biceps contracts and gets shorter. Mark two: at the same time, the triceps relaxes.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:00.785
Mark three: they are an antagonistic pair. They work against each other: as one contracts, the other relaxes.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:09.374
Mark four: the biceps pulls on its tendon. The tendon does not stretch, so it pulls the radius, and the forearm is raised.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:17.191
Here is the full answer, in four points. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:31.523
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say the triceps pushes the arm down. Muscles never push. The triceps contracts and pulls. Also, the two muscles never contract at the same time.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:43.011
Another mistake is to say the tendon is elastic, or that the biceps pulls the humerus. The tendon does not stretch, and the biceps pulls the radius in the forearm.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:53.759
Your turn. A swimmer straightens her arm at the elbow to push back through the water. Explain how her muscles straighten her arm. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:09.879
Answer. Straightening means the triceps contracts and gets shorter. The biceps relaxes. They are an antagonistic pair. The triceps pulls on its tendon, which pulls the ulna, so the forearm straightens at the elbow.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:20.589
So, every time: decide the movement. Say which muscle contracts and which relaxes. Use the word antagonistic. Then say the tendon pulls the bone.
